1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,939
Another internationalization
library? Fair question.

2
00:00:03,939 --> 00:00:08,636
Here's our answer, you need translation
right where you already work.

3
00:00:08,636 --> 00:00:13,332
In your JSX, in Vite, in VS Code.
Your tools should be helping you...

4
00:00:16,800 --> 00:00:20,011
So we built it into all
three. viteTranslate.

5
00:00:20,011 --> 00:00:22,864
Write the sentence, get the translation.

6
00:00:24,267 --> 00:00:27,832
Write the sentence in JSX,
just as it reads. The key?

7
00:00:27,832 --> 00:00:32,406
viteTranslate makes it, at build
time or from the VS Code extension.

8
00:00:32,406 --> 00:00:33,617
Nothing to manage.

9
00:00:35,600 --> 00:00:40,590
And here's VS Code. An official extension
brings translation into the editor.

10
00:00:40,590 --> 00:00:44,543
Every sentence with its status,
highlighted the way you like.

11
00:00:46,333 --> 00:00:49,783
In the browser, it's already
there. Not in French yet?

12
00:00:49,783 --> 00:00:53,615
You get the English, with a
marker that only developers see.

13
00:00:53,615 --> 00:00:57,320
It never reaches production.
Never a blank, never a crash.

14
00:00:59,300 --> 00:01:00,479
Let's try a build.

15
00:01:00,479 --> 00:01:04,932
The Vite plugin brings every language
file back in sync, on its own.

16
00:01:04,932 --> 00:01:09,451
Extraction is part of Vite's lifecycle,
no extra command to remember.

17
00:01:09,451 --> 00:01:13,708
And French gets a chunk of its
own, loaded only when it's needed.

18
00:01:15,700 --> 00:01:17,907
And in production, it travels light.

19
00:01:17,907 --> 00:01:20,973
Zero runtime dependencies,
five kilobytes gzipped.

20
00:01:20,973 --> 00:01:25,879
ICU plurals and dates come precompiled,
so the browser gets values ready to use.

21
00:01:27,267 --> 00:01:30,785
The panel already knows what's
waiting for a translation.

22
00:01:30,785 --> 00:01:34,983
Click the new one, then French, and
the file opens right on the key.

23
00:01:34,983 --> 00:01:37,390
Type, save, and there it is, in French.

24
00:01:42,167 --> 00:01:46,472
A thousand keys? We built in
an LLM too, any one you like.

25
00:01:46,472 --> 00:01:51,297
It shows the cost, asks before
spending, and checks every result.

26
00:01:52,900 --> 00:01:55,572
Sometimes, simple is better.

27
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:07,630
Ten features, one package,
built to work together.

28
00:02:07,630 --> 00:02:14,238
Lingui, FormatJS and i18next are great libraries,
and the full comparison is in the README.

29
00:02:14,238 --> 00:02:18,086
We chose to put it all in
one place. Where you write.

30
00:02:21,567 --> 00:02:26,041
viteTranslate. On npm, in the VS
Code Marketplace, and on GitHub.

31
00:02:26,041 --> 00:02:29,414
You write the sentence,
we take care of the rest.
